images:
  - file: Bizansist_touchup.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Bizansist_touchup.jpg/330px-Bizansist_touchup.jpg
    width: 2516
    height: 970
    caption: Aerial view of Byzantine Constantinople and the Propontis (Sea of Marmara)
  - file: Column_of_Constantine.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Column_of_Constantine.jpg/250px-Column_of_Constantine.jpg
    width: 3072
    height: 4608
    caption: The Column of Constantine, built by Constantine I in 330 to commemorate
      the establishment of Constantinople as the new capital of the Roman Empire
  - file: Horses_of_Basilica_San_Marco.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Horses_of_Basilica_San_Marco.jpg/250px-Horses_of_Basilica_San_Marco.jpg
    width: 3859
    height: 2737
    caption: The four bronze horses that were once in the Hippodrome of
      Constantinople are today put on display in Venice.
  - file: Irenekirken.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Irenekirken.jpg/250px-Irenekirken.jpg
    width: 1332
    height: 1798
    caption: "A simple cross: an example of iconoclast art in the Hagia Irene Church
      in Istanbul, which was secularized and today serves as a museum and
      concert hall."
  - file: Constantine_I_Hagia_Sophia.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Constantine_I_Hagia_Sophia.jpg/250px-Constantine_I_Hagia_Sophia.jpg
    width: 688
    height: 702
    caption: Emperor Constantine I presents a representation of the city of
      Constantinople as tribute to an enthroned Mary and Christ Child in this
      church mosaic. Hagia Sophia, c. 1000.
  - file: Istanbul_Hagia_Irene_IMG_8067_1920.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Istanbul_Hagia_Irene_IMG_8067_1920.jpg/250px-Istanbul_Hagia_Irene_IMG_8067_1920.jpg
    width: 2756
    height: 1920
    caption: Hagia Irene is a Greek Eastern Orthodox Church located in the outer
      courtyard of Topkapı Palace in Istanbul. It is one of the few churches in
      Istanbul that has not been converted into a mosque.
  - file: Istambul_cokol1RB.JPG
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Istambul_cokol1RB.JPG/250px-Istambul_cokol1RB.JPG
    width: 1280
    height: 960
    caption: Theodosius I was the last Roman emperor who ruled over an undivided
      empire (detail from the Obelisk at the Hippodrome of Constantinople).
  - file: Bozdoģan_Kemeri_-_panoramio.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Bozdo%C4%A3an_Kemeri_-_panoramio.jpg/250px-Bozdo%C4%A3an_Kemeri_-_panoramio.jpg
    width: 3908
    height: 2612
    caption: Aqueduct of Valens, completed by Roman emperor Valens in the late 4th
      century AD
  - file: Map_of_Constantinople,_Buondelmonti.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Map_of_Constantinople%2C_Buondelmonti.jpg/250px-Map_of_Constantinople%2C_Buondelmonti.jpg
    width: 2291
    height: 3320
    caption: The map of Constantinople (1422) by Florentine cartographer Cristoforo
      Buondelmonti is the oldest surviving map of the city, and the only one
      that predates the Turkish conquest of it in 1453.
  - file: Hagia_Sophia_Mars_2013.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Hagia_Sophia_Mars_2013.jpg/250px-Hagia_Sophia_Mars_2013.jpg
    width: 5514
    height: 3681
    caption: Emperor Justinian I commissioned the current Hagia Sophia after the
      previous one was destroyed in the Nika riots of 532. It was converted into
      a mosque in 1453 when the Ottoman Empire captured the city, while it
      served as a museum from 1935 to 2020, when it was converted back to a
      mosque.
  - file: Walls_of_Constantinople.JPG
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Walls_of_Constantinople.JPG/250px-Walls_of_Constantinople.JPG
    width: 1920
    height: 1080
    caption: Restored section of the fortifications (Theodosian Walls) that
      protected Constantinople during the medieval period
  - file: Chora_Church_Constantinople_2007_panorama_002.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Chora_Church_Constantinople_2007_panorama_002.jpg/250px-Chora_Church_Constantinople_2007_panorama_002.jpg
    width: 2460
    height: 1456
    caption: Chora Church, a medieval Byzantine Greek Orthodox church in Istanbul's
      Edirnekapı quarter, was converted into Kariye Mosque in 1500 and again in
      2024, after serving as the Chora Museum from 1945 to 2020.
  - file: Imperial_Gate_mosaic_in_Hagia_Sophia.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Imperial_Gate_mosaic_in_Hagia_Sophia.jpg/250px-Imperial_Gate_mosaic_in_Hagia_Sophia.jpg
    width: 3072
    height: 2048
    caption: Emperor Leo VI (886–912) adoring Jesus Christ. Mosaic above the
      Imperial Gate in the Hagia Sophia.
  - file: Christ_Pantocrator_mosaic_from_Hagia_Sophia_2744_x_2900_pixels_3.1_MB.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Christ_Pantocrator_mosaic_from_Hagia_Sophia_2744_x_2900_pixels_3.1_MB.jpg/250px-Christ_Pantocrator_mosaic_from_Hagia_Sophia_2744_x_2900_pixels_3.1_MB.jpg
    width: 2744
    height: 2900
    caption: One of the most famous of the surviving Byzantine mosaics of the Hagia
      Sophia in Constantinople – the image of Christ Pantocrator on the walls of
      the upper southern gallery, Christ being flanked by the Virgin Mary and
      John the Baptist, c. 1261
  - file: Pammakaristos_Church_Main_Dome_mosaic_Christ_Pantocrator_in_2010_01_9607.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Pammakaristos_Church_Main_Dome_mosaic_Christ_Pantocrator_in_2010_01_9607.jpg/250px-Pammakaristos_Church_Main_Dome_mosaic_Christ_Pantocrator_in_2010_01_9607.jpg
    width: 4256
    height: 2832
    caption: Mosaic of Jesus in Pammakaristos Church, Istanbul
  - file: Byzantium@1180.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Byzantium%401180.jpg/250px-Byzantium%401180.jpg
    width: 801
    height: 370
    caption: The Byzantine Empire under Manuel I, c. 1180
  - file: Pammakaristos_Church_Mosaic_of_St._Anthony_the_Great_in_2010_30_9593.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Pammakaristos_Church_Mosaic_of_St._Anthony_the_Great_in_2010_30_9593.jpg/250px-Pammakaristos_Church_Mosaic_of_St._Anthony_the_Great_in_2010_30_9593.jpg
    width: 4256
    height: 2832
    caption: Pammakaristos Church mosaic of Saint Anthony, the desert Father
  - file: Eugène_Delacroix_-_Prise_de_Constantinople_par_les_croisés_(12_avril_1204).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Eug%C3%A8ne_Delacroix_-_Prise_de_Constantinople_par_les_crois%C3%A9s_%2812_avril_1204%29.jpg/250px-Eug%C3%A8ne_Delacroix_-_Prise_de_Constantinople_par_les_crois%C3%A9s_%2812_avril_1204%29.jpg
    width: 2223
    height: 1820
    caption: The Entry of the Crusaders into Constantinople, by Eugène Delacroix, 1840
  - file: Byzantium1204.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Byzantium1204.png/250px-Byzantium1204.png
    width: 512
    height: 357
    caption: The Latin Empire, Empire of Nicaea, Empire of Trebizond, and the
      Despotate of Epirus. The borders are very uncertain.
  - file: Fethiye_Museum_9620.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Fethiye_Museum_9620.jpg/250px-Fethiye_Museum_9620.jpg
    width: 1600
